<http://example.org/sen0414/ch10/diagrams> a owl:Ontology ; rdfs:label "SEN0414 ch10 narrative diagrams - ABox"@en ; owl:versionInfo "1.0.0" ; dcterms:identifier "sen0414_ch10_diagrams_abox_v1_0_0" ;
    rdfs:comment "The chapter's authored diagrams as cme:NarrativeDiagram individuals: generated by sen0414_page_data_v2_6_0.py from sen0414_ch10_diagrams_v1_0_0.py, typed by the CME narrative-to-diagram mapping (a module of cme_standards_adoption_v1_3_0.ttl, CME 0.16.0)."@en ; owl:imports <http://example.org/cme/narrative-diagram> .

chx:Diagram_ChoosingAPathOperation a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "What kind of thing a path operation does"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:Workflow ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:ActivityDiagram ; cme:readFrom "chapter 10, the filepaths and folders sections: Path() joins names into a path object without touching a disk, with_suffix and relative_to build a new path the same way, exists and is_file ask the file system a question without changing anything, and mkdir and makedirs create a folder on disk" ; cme:elementCount 11 ;
    cme:accompanies "PathConstruction" ; cme:accompanies "PathDerivation" ; cme:accompanies "PathValidity" ; cme:accompanies "MakingFolders" ; rdfs:comment "the explanations describe what a programmer decides, in order, when one line of code calling a path method is written - does it only compute a new path object, does it only ask the file system a question, or does it change a file or folder on disk - with a different answer reached on each branch: a workflow with choices on the way"@en .
chx:Diagram_ChoosingAnOpenMode a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "Choosing the mode to open a file in"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:Workflow ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:ActivityDiagram ; cme:readFrom "chapter 10, the opened files and whole file methods sections: open() reads in mode r by default, writes after emptying the file in mode w, appends in mode a, and creates a new file and fails if it exists in mode x, and text or binary data decides whether the file is opened in text or binary mode" ; cme:elementCount 14 ;
    cme:accompanies "OpenAndModes" ; cme:accompanies "PlainVersusBinaryFiles" ; rdfs:comment "the explanations describe what a programmer decides, in order, when open() is called - is the file read or written, should an existing file be emptied or kept, and is the data text or binary - with a different mode chosen on each branch: a workflow with choices on the way"@en .
chx:Diagram_FileFromOpenToClosed a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "A file object's life from opened to closed"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:LifeCycle ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:StateDiagram ; cme:readFrom "chapter 10, the opened files and automatic closing sections: open() returns a file object; write() and read() act on it while it is open; close() ends its use, and a with statement closes it automatically on every way out of its block; a closed file object raises ValueError if it is read or written again" ; cme:elementCount 8 ;
    cme:accompanies "OpenAndModes" ; cme:accompanies "WriteMethod" ; cme:accompanies "ReadMethods" ; cme:accompanies "CloseMethod" ; cme:accompanies "WithStatement" ; cme:accompanies "ClosedFileError" ; rdfs:comment "the explanations describe the states one file object passes through on its way from being opened to being closed - opened, written or read, then closed - and the operation that moves it from one state to the next, including the with statement's own automatic move to closed: a life cycle"@en .
chx:Diagram_ShelfRoundTrip a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "A value's round trip through a shelf"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:Interaction ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:SequenceDiagram ; cme:readFrom "chapter 10, the shelves section: shelve.open() opens a shelf like a dictionary, a value is stored under a key and the shelf is closed, and a later shelve.open() of the same file reads the value back by the same key, though a shelf cannot see a mutable value changed in place unless it is assigned back or writeback is used" ; cme:elementCount 7 ;
    cme:accompanies "ShelveOpen" ; cme:accompanies "ShelfAsDictionary" ; cme:accompanies "ShelfWriteback" ; rdfs:comment "the passage is an exchange between parties in an order that matters - a value is stored under a key, the shelf is closed, the shelf is reopened, and the same key reads the value back - an interaction"@en .
chx:Diagram_QuizGeneratorUses a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "What Project 4's quiz generator lets a user do"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:SetOfUses ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:UseCaseDiagram ; cme:readFrom "chapter 10, the quiz program section: the program keeps the states and capitals in a dictionary, shuffles the states and the wrong answers, writes a quiz file and an answer key file for each quiz, and a student who takes the quiz can be marked against the answer key" ; cme:elementCount 10 ;
    cme:accompanies "QuizData" ; cme:accompanies "QuizFiles" ; cme:accompanies "QuizOutputDiffers" ; rdfs:comment "the passage lists what the program and a person each do with the files it writes - keep the data, shuffle the order, write a quiz file, write an answer key file, take the quiz, mark it against the key - a set of uses by actors, not a sequence"@en .
chx:Diagram_FilesAtAGlance a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "Reading and writing files at a glance: four branches"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:TopicAndParts ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:MindMap ; cme:readFrom "chapter 10, the opening of each of its four main branches: a path names where a file lies, a folder is created and searched, a text file is read and written whole or opened as a file object, and saved data outlives the program in a shelf or a json file" ; cme:elementCount 18 ;
    cme:accompanies "Filepaths" ; cme:accompanies "Folders" ; cme:accompanies "TextFiles" ; cme:accompanies "SavedData" ; rdfs:comment "the section classifies one topic, reading and writing files, by what each branch is about and lists examples under each - a topic and its parts, laid out to remember, with the one idea that holds for every part stated once at the root"@en .
